mit. If those amounts were spread as in the band immediately below, 1.4 contracts would be expected. The observed-to-expected ratio is 16.4, with a 95% confidence interval of 6.8 to 45.3. December accounts for 0% of the contracts just under the limit, compared with 5% of all its services and supplies contracts. The awardee with the most contracts just under the limit, called awardee A here, has 7. Contract [1], for 14,900 euros, was awarded in June 2025.\nWhat it cannot show: The concentration of contracts near the limit threshold does not indicate improper conduct. It could reflect market pricing, budget constraints, or specific needs. Statistical patterns alone do not prove wrongdoing in procurement processes.\nWhat can be checked: Check the dates and amounts of contracts [1] and [2] to see if they align with the body’s stated needs. Review awardee A’s contracts to determine if multiple contracts served the same purpose. Look for patterns in the distribution of contracts across different sectors or services.","origen_es":"modelo","origen_en":"modelo","modelo":"Qwen3-4B (Q4_K_M, llama.cpp)","regla":"ss","citas":["Y%2FMeD5eeWmk7u6%2B%2FR7DUoA%3D%3D","FbtZ91EOSJhWhbmkna2nXQ%3D%3D","zpK6wJrCGCiKeVWTb9Scog%3D%3D"],"generado":"2026-11-15"}}